Picture \par
\par
In John Webster\rquote s The Duchess of Malfi, the eponymous heroine hides her desire for Antonio from her two controlling brothers. Her twin, Ferdinand, warns her to fear exposure: \lquote Your darkest actions \f1\emdash  nay, your privatest thoughts,/ Will come to light\rquote  (1.2.231\endash 32). The play frames light not as salvation or as a symbol of hope but as a means of control wielded by Ferdinand to dominate his sister in a \lquote culture of surveillance\rquote  (Coleman 2010, p. 87). \par
\par
It is surprising that the Duchess of Malfi is threatened with light. In early modern literature, darkness, not light, is usually framed as a threat: it is associated with sinister, even supernatural danger, with secrets, and with grief. For example, in Shakespeare\rquote s King Lear, \lquote dark\rquote  is often paired with menacing adjectives such as \lquote dark and comfortless\rquote  (3.7.84), \lquote dark and vicious\rquote  (5.3.170), \lquote cheerless, dark, and deadly\rquote  (5.3.288). As this article argues, however, Malfi does not offer a straightforward binary of light or darkness. Instead, these symbols shift and even invert, reflecting the play\rquote s insistence that even when viewed in the clearest daylight, appearances are not to be trusted. \par
\par
Brightness and concealment \par
\par
In The Duchess of Malfi, Webster plays not only with light and dark but also with colour and concealment, to undermine an audience\rquote s trust in what they see. Colour, for instance, is unstable within the play, prone to changing or fading. The Duchess asks Antonio, both as a joke and with a hint of regret, \lquote Doth not the colour of my hair \rquote gin to change?\rquote  (3.2.57). Colour\rquote s instability proves more sinister as the play progresses. When the Duchess is killed, Bosola fantasises that he can undo death\rquote s finality by tinting her pale lips with his blood: \lquote Upon thy pale lips I will melt my heart,/ To store them with fresh colour\rquote  (4.2.333\endash 34). That change of colour proves impossible. Her lips will not change from deathly white back to vital red. Whereas a change of colour (her greying hair) once meant natural ageing, colour change now means death that comes unnaturally to the young. \par
\par
Light, like colour, also shifts in its associations. The Duchess is associated with light but it does not simply carry clich\f0\'e9d connotations of goodness, beauty or truth. The slipperiness of light as a metaphor is evident when Antonio describes the Duchess as someone who \lquote stains the time past, light the time to come\rquote  (1.2.127), a line also found in Webster\rquote s tribute to Prince Henry Frederick, the son of James I who died in 1612 aged 18. The image, shared between both the Duchess and the young prince, makes what appears a simple compliment seem murky: suddenly, the line is not just the praise of a lover but a politically charged sentiment. As Leah Marcus puts it, \lquote in creating his Duchess, Webster was also evoking a set of ideals that appeared to many in England to have died with Henry\rquote  (Marcus 2009, p. 9). Antonio\rquote s compliment therefore conceals a pointed political allusion that compares the Duchess to the now-lost Protestant prince. \par
\par
Light is a source of horror as much as a source of comfort or truth. It is light that reveals the dead man\rquote s hand presented to the Duchess as a trick; she calls for them in the darkness (\lquote Lights! Oh, horrible!\rquote  [4.1.52]), and Ferdinand finishes her line of pentameter by using light as a threat (\lquote Let her have lights enough\rquote  [4.1.52]). When Bosola brings in the light the Duchess has called for, it does not offer relief. Instead, it reveals the horrible sight of the severed hand. The Duchess is shown \lquote the artificial figures of Antonio and his Children, appearing as if they were dead\rquote  (4.1.54). Tormented by sights that are not real, she is \lquote plagued in art\rquote  (4.1.109). Neither light nor darkness reveals the truth of Ferdinand\rquote s trick to her. This crucial moment underscores the falsehood and corruption in Ferdinand and his court. \par
\par
Performance by candlelight \par
\par
Thinking about light and colour in Webster\rquote s tragedy also means considering the practicalities of his staging. Many key moments in the play hinge on the concealment made possible by darkness. The play calls for four separate \lquote dark scenes\rquote , which could be performed even in outdoor playhouses like the Globe Theatre (with an audience simply imagining the darkness) or the indoor Blackfriars playhouse (where it would have been played by candlelight). From its title page, we know The Duchess of Malfi was performed at both. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Jeremy Lopez describes \lquote dark scenes\rquote  as a convention of the early modern stage, created through a mixture of dialogue (characters onstage describe the darkness), props (characters carry candles or lanterns), and performance (actors physically demonstrate they cannot see through the shadows) (Lopez 2003, pp. 103\f1\endash 4). The birth of the Duchess\rquote s child is concealed at night when Antonio tries to disguise her labour cries as the shrieks of an owl. She is tricked into taking a dead man\rquote s hand and into believing she sees the corpses of her children while imprisoned in the dark. Her echo sounds out through the shadows in an abandoned cloister, with Antonio left to wonder whether or not he has seen his wife\rquote s ghost. And at last, Antonio enacts his revenge in a \lquote dark scene\rquote  that leads to his own death. Webster\rquote s play investigates both darkness of the soul and material darkness on stage. \par
\par
Imagery on the digital stage \par
\par
How then do these concepts transfer to the digital stage in our modern age? The digital stage is an intangible space that exists only on-screen. During the pandemic lockdown in 2020, I co-directed a digital production of The Duchess of Malfi with Natasha Rickman for Creation Theatre. Actors scattered across the country performed live on-screen, creating the stage, often from their bedrooms. (This process is discussed further in a podcast: {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://tinyurl.com/2xnn5xt4 }}{\fldrslt{https://tinyurl.com/2xnn5xt4\ul0\cf0}}}}\f1\fs22 .) \par
\par
Early in rehearsals, we described the twins, Ferdinand and the Duchess, as being alike and different, represented by the moon and the sun. We were inspired by the moment at which Ferdinand threatens the Duchess with a \lquote general eclipse\rquote  (2.5.79), an end to her sunlight. This metaphor soon becomes literal, when Ferdinand refuses to see his sister unless she is in darkness (\lquote for indeed,/ You were too much i\rquote th\rquote light\rquote  [4.1.40- 41]). Finally, Ferdinand reacts to the Duchess\rquote s dead body as if he were looking directly into the sun: \lquote Cover her face. Mine eyes dazzle; she died young\rquote  (4.2.254). Ferdinand cannot look at the evidence of his sister\rquote s murder; the light of truth is too much, too painful. \par
\par
In our production, we drew out lunar and solar themes through colour and costuming. The Duchess (Annabelle May Terry), for instance, wore earrings that showed both sun and moon. Ferdinand (Dharmesh Patel) began the play wearing sunglasses, as if already dazzled by his sister\rquote s radiance. The glasses jarred with his heavy fur coat, which foreshadowed Ferdinand\rquote s later belief that he is turning into a werewolf. In her happy moments, the Duchess wore sunglasses too, but her association with sunlight slowly faded throughout our production. During Act 3 Scene 5, in which she is arrested by Bosola, our Duchess wore a heavy camel-coloured coat and scarf, as if no longer reached by the sun. \par
\par
Primary colours \par
\par
As well as its solar associations, our yellow palette was inspired by 1960s and 70s Italian \lquote Giallo\rquote  films, which are known for setting violent scenes in bright, stylised primary colours. Our production was set in 1970s Italy and made in Giallo style, complete with bright colours, exaggerated violence and an Italian pop soundtrack. The sexually charged pulp-fiction novels that inspired Giallo films had yellow covers (giallo is Italian for yellow), and so our Duchess\rquote s dress was yellow as an ironic comment on the accusations of sexual \lquote sin\rquote  she faces. \par
\par
Yet colour, both in our production and in Webster\rquote s text, is deliberately misleading. He reminds his audience that colour is easily used to distract and conceal. When Bosola reveals that he has witnessed the murder of Julia, he confronts her killer, the Cardinal, with the question, \lquote And wherefore should you lay fair marble colours/ Upon your rotten purposes to me?\rquote  (5.2.282\endash  83). Marble comes in many colours \emdash  yellow, black, white, orange etc. \emdash  so Webster uses this line to suggest colour of all kinds can conceal. Bathed in bright colours, our production of Malfi looked glamorous and full of life, but, like fair marble or a yellow book cover, the colours concealed the darkness below the surface. \par
\par
To underscore the instability of the Duchess\rquote s \lquote fair\rquote  yellow world, our production delivered Ferdinand\rquote s promised eclipse. The Duchess\rquote s fall at the hands of her brothers was reflected by a world submerged in blue. The blood smeared on the Duchess\rquote s face, transferred from the dead man\rquote s hand she is given, was only visible under the ultraviolet (UV) light we used to create the scene. This UV light reflected Webster\rquote s use of light and colour: at once concealing and revealing, the UV showed the blood as a glowing, neon blue against the Duchess\rquote s skin, exposing traces of violence that were invisible in both darkness and ordinary white light. \par
\par
 \par
\par
In the final act, in which Bosola seeks revenge for the Duchess, the colour palette, once so sunny and yellow, changed even more significantly, in part due to the use of coloured filters laid on top of the online feed through a software vision mixer. Scenes shifted sharply between red, our symbol for violence, and blue, our symbol for grief. The scene was blue, for instance, as Antonio and Delio hear an echo that might just be the Duchess through the darkness. This then shifted to a dark red, representing not only the shadows of the final \lquote dark scene\rquote  in which the characters cannot properly see one another onstage, but also the blood that will soon be shed. It is only at the very end of the play, when all those onstage are dead apart from Delio, that the colour yellow returned. Here, with a change to Webster\rquote s original ending, I adapted the script so that the Duchess\rquote s surviving child would be not a son but a baby daughter. As the final soliloquy was delivered, in the redness of the final scene\rquote s bloodbath, that daughter was wrapped in a yellow blanket, recalling the bright colour of her mother\rquote s dress. \par
\par
Eclipse and fade out \par
\par
Performing Malfi in a digital context, we were able to experiment with both light and colour, creating a stylised production in which the Duchess\rquote s sunny world slowly darkened. Her warm yellows, like the \lquote fair marble colours\rquote  (5.2.282\endash 83) Bosola describes, were used to cover the misogyny and corruption at the heart of the play, until all turned blood red and faded to black. \par
